PHONE

TITLE
Java to C Converter, with Support for Ptolemy II SDF Actors

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT 
The synchronous dataflow domain of Ptolemy II is excellent for representing 
signal processing systems. However, because the code is written in Java and
because there is an overhead involved in communication between the
signal processing blocks (or SDF actors), the performance is inherently
not optimal. In addition, such a system cannot be easily ported to a
DSP chip, as there are few Java compilers and Java virtual machines for DSP's.

DSP's do have C compilers, which frequently generate optimized code. So
the goal of the project is to generate C code from a Ptolemy II SDF system.
To reach that goal, we must have the ability to translate ordinary Java code 
into C code as well, which would be useful to many Java programmers. Once
we have that abiility, we will optimize the communication between SDF actors,
to produce C code that is reasonably efficient.  

